 ## Overview
-This family encompasses volume rendering techniques that offload the computational workload of ray casting and compositing to parallel hardware accelerators, primarily GPUs, using shading languages (GLSL/HLSL) or compute APIs (CUDA/OpenCL) [S1]. It solves the performance bottleneck of CPU-based volume rendering by leveraging massive parallelism to process pixels or voxels simultaneously [G]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Tiled Parallel Compositing](1192-tiled-parallel-compositing.md) by focusing on the low-level mapping of rendering tasks to hardware execution units (shaders, cores, or warps) rather than high-level algorithmic partitioning strategies, and differs from [Multi-core BONO Tree Traversal and Ray Casting with Dynamic Tile Grouping](2801-multi-core-bono-tree-traversal-and-ray-casting-with-dynamic-.md) by targeting GPU architectures rather than multi-core CPUs.
